[38;5;231m⏺[39m The backticks in my commit message got command-substituted by the shell. Amending with a clean message:

  [38;5;246mRan [1m1[0m[38;5;246m shell command [39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Committed on branch [38;5;153mfix/limiter-off-by-one[39m (a417cef, amended) — one line changed, [38;5;153msrc/ratelimit/limiter.py:23[39m. Tests
  pass (2/2), ruff clean. Not pushed.

  My decisions on the three notes:

  [1m1. Off-by-one — implemented.[0m Real bug, already caught by [38;5;153mtests/test_limiter.py::test_does_not_exceed_limit[39m, which was
  failing with [38;5;153massert 4 == 3[39m before the change.

  [1m2. [38;5;153mtime.time()[39m — declined.[0m The monotonic clock is load-bearing, not incidental. [38;5;153mtime.time()[39m can step backwards on NTP
  correction, which pushes [38;5;153mcutoff[39m backwards so entries never expire and the limiter wedges shut; it can step forwards,
  which clears the window at once and lets a full burst through. Trading a correctness property in a gateway rate
  limiter for timestamp aesthetics isn't a trade I'd make.

  The reviewer's actual need — lining up with wall-clock log lines — is separable from the window arithmetic. When
  something in this package starts logging, the fix is to emit [38;5;153mtime.time()[39m at log time, or store a wall-clock field
  alongside the monotonic one. I didn't add that field now because nothing logs yet; it would be an unused attribute.

  [1m3. Storage-backend interface — declined.[0m Nothing in the repo touches Redis, and [38;5;153mallow()[39m is the entire consumer
  surface. The deeper problem is that a Redis sliding window isn't a deque with different method names: correctness
  there needs [38;5;153mZADD[39m/[38;5;153mZREMRANGEBYSCORE[39m in a Lua script for atomicity, with the clock server-side rather than in the Python
  process. An interface extracted from the deque alone would encode "read list, mutate in process, write back" — a
  non-atomic shape Redis can't honor under concurrency, so it would be rewritten the day Redis lands. Better to write
  the Redis limiter for real and extract the interface from two working implementations.

  If Redis is actually scheduled, say so and I'll start on that implementation rather than an abstraction over one
  backend.

